    <section class="section">
      <div class="container two-col">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2>Fixes that don't need a tow</h2>
          <p>Roadside assistance is for the problems that can, in principle, be sorted where your vehicle is standing — no loading, no trailer, no trip to a garage required. It sits apart from <a href="breakdown-recovery-aberdeen.html">breakdown recovery</a>, which is what happens once we know the vehicle genuinely can't be made driveable on the spot. Plenty of call-outs turn out to be one of these quicker fixes, so it's always worth describing the problem and letting us judge which service fits.</p>

          <h2>What roadside assistance typically covers</h2>
          <ul class="checklist">
            <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg> Jump starts for a flat battery.</li>
            <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg> Tyre changes, where a roadworthy spare and the right tools are available.</li>
            <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg> Lockout assistance if you're locked out of your own vehicle.</li>
            <li><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg> Running out of fuel, with the right fuel type brought to you.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>How it works</h2>
          <p>Call and describe exactly what's happening — what the vehicle is doing, or not doing, and anything you've already tried. We'll talk you through what to expect and send someone out equipped to deal with it at the roadside. If, once we're there, it turns out the fault is more serious than first thought, we won't leave you stuck: we'll move straight into recovery instead.</p>

          <div class="callout">
            <p><strong>Safety first.</strong> If you're stopped on a fast or busy road, don't attempt any roadside fix yourself, and don't stand between your vehicle and moving traffic. Get well clear, keep hazard lights on, and call us before doing anything else.</p>
          </div>

          <h2>Roadside assistance vs breakdown recovery</h2>
          <p>The dividing line is simple: if the vehicle can be made safely driveable where it's stopped, that's roadside assistance. If it can't — a fault that needs a workshop, or damage that means it shouldn't be driven — that's a job for <a href="breakdown-recovery-aberdeen.html">breakdown recovery</a>. You don't need to work out which one applies before calling; just explain what's wrong and we'll take it from there.</p>
        </div>

